# EphCore.TopocentricMotion — Retrograde Loops & Stations

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:eph_core, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
  ],
  config: [
    eph_core: [
      # Planetary DE440s only. Avoid config_path: :eph_core here — that loads
      # config.exs asteroid kernels before SPK.Server starts.
      kernel_base_dir: Path.expand("../../priv", __DIR__),
      spk_kernels: ["de440s.bsp"]
    ]
  ]
)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
```

## Overview

A planet's eastward march along the ecliptic occasionally slows, stops, and
reverses — **retrograde motion** — an illusion born of Earth overtaking (or
being overtaken by) another world. The instants where the apparent longitude
rate crosses zero are the **stations** (retrograde station and direct station).

This tour uses two pieces of the engine:

* `SnapshotPipeline.observe/4` — its `motion` map already reports each body's
  geocentric `ecliptic_lon_rate_deg_per_day` and a `retrograde` flag.
* `EphCore.TopocentricMotion` — `ecliptic_lon_rates/4` recomputes the rate in the
  **observer-topocentric** frame (central difference on `observe/4` longitudes),
  and `augment/5` merges that back onto a geocentric motion map. This is the
  frame used to refine station times (rate zero-crossings).

Retrograde is pure parallax geometry — and the rate curve makes the illusion quantitative.

**How to open:** Install [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/), open this `.livemd`,
evaluate the setup cell. Kernels must be under `eph_core/priv/ephemeris/`. The
default window catches **Mars's 2024–2025 retrograde**.

## Helpers

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl
alias EphCore.SnapshotPipeline
alias EphCore.TopocentricMotion
alias AstroUtils.Angle

:ok
```

## Ready check

```elixir
Kino.Markdown.new("""
**EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` loaded. Ready to scan.
""")
```

## Body & window

```elixir
body_in =
  Kino.Input.select(
    "Body",
    for(b <- [:mars, :mercury, :venus, :jupiter, :saturn], do: {b, b})
  )

start_in = Kino.Input.text("Start date (UTC)", default: "2024-11-01")
end_in = Kino.Input.text("End date (UTC)", default: "2025-04-01")
step_in = Kino.Input.number("Step (days)", default: 2)

Kino.Layout.grid([body_in, start_in, end_in, step_in], columns: 2)
```

```elixir
body = Kino.Input.read(body_in)
start_date = Date.from_iso8601!(String.trim(Kino.Input.read(start_in)))
end_date = Date.from_iso8601!(String.trim(Kino.Input.read(end_in)))
step_days = trunc(Kino.Input.read(step_in))

# Minneapolis observer; retrograde is a geocentric phenomenon so location barely matters.
location = %{lat: 44.9778, lon: -93.2650, height: 250.0}

{:ok, start_dt, _} = DateTime.from_iso8601(Date.to_iso8601(start_date) <> "T06:00:00Z")
{:ok, end_dt, _} = DateTime.from_iso8601(Date.to_iso8601(end_date) <> "T06:00:00Z")

total_days = DateTime.diff(end_dt, start_dt, :second) |> div(86_400)
sample_dts = for d <- 0..total_days//step_days, do: DateTime.add(start_dt, d * 86_400, :second)

Kino.Markdown.new("Scanning **#{body}** across **#{length(sample_dts)}** samples (#{start_date} → #{end_date}, every #{step_days} d).")
```

## 1 — Scan the longitude & rate curves

Fan the samples out across cores with `Task.async_stream`. Each sample reads the
geocentric ecliptic longitude, latitude, and the pipeline's own rate + retrograde
flag.

```elixir
{scan_us, scan} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    sample_dts
    |> Task.async_stream(
      fn dt ->
        {:ok, obs} = SnapshotPipeline.observe(dt, location, [body])
        ssp = obs.solar_system_positions[body]
        motion = obs.motion[body]

        %{
          date: DateTime.to_date(dt) |> Date.to_iso8601(),
          dt: dt,
          lon_deg: ssp.ecliptic_longitude,
          lat_deg: ssp.ecliptic_latitude,
          rate_deg_day: motion.ecliptic_lon_rate_deg_per_day,
          retrograde: motion.retrograde
        }
      end,
      ordered: true,
      timeout: 60_000
    )
    |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, row} -> row end)
  end)

Kino.render(Kino.Markdown.new("Scanned in **#{Float.round(scan_us / 1000, 1)} ms**."))

table_rows =
  Enum.map(scan, fn r ->
    %{
      date: r.date,
      lon_deg: Float.round(r.lon_deg, 4),
      lat_deg: Float.round(r.lat_deg, 4),
      rate_deg_day: Float.round(r.rate_deg_day, 5),
      retrograde: r.retrograde
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(table_rows, name: "#{body} longitude & rate scan")
```

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 620, height: 260, title: "#{body}: ecliptic longitude rate (deg/day)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(table_rows)
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:line, point: false)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "date", type: :temporal, title: nil)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "rate_deg_day", type: :quantitative, title: "Rate (°/day)")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "retrograde", type: :nominal, title: "Retrograde?"),
  # Zero line: the rate crossings are the stations.
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:rule, color: "black", stroke_dash: [4, 4])
  |> Vl.encode(:y, datum: 0)
])
```

When the rate dips **below zero**, the planet is moving retrograde (westward);
the zero crossings are the stations.

## 2 — The retrograde loop

Plotting ecliptic longitude against latitude traces the actual **loop** the
planet appears to draw against the stars — the signature S-curve or teardrop.

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 460, height: 380, title: "#{body}: apparent path (ecliptic lon vs lat)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(table_rows)
|> Vl.mark(:line, point: true)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "lon_deg", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false], title: "Ecliptic longitude (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "lat_deg", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false], title: "Ecliptic latitude (°)")
|> Vl.encode_field(:order, "date", type: :temporal)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "retrograde", type: :nominal, title: "Retrograde?")
```

## 3 — Find the stations (rate sign changes)

Walk consecutive samples; where the sign of the rate flips, linearly interpolate
the zero crossing to estimate the station instant and the longitude it happens at.

```elixir
stations =
  scan
  |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
  |> Enum.flat_map(fn [a, b] ->
    if a.rate_deg_day == 0.0 or a.rate_deg_day * b.rate_deg_day < 0.0 do
      # Fraction of the interval where rate hits zero.
      frac = a.rate_deg_day / (a.rate_deg_day - b.rate_deg_day)
      secs = DateTime.diff(b.dt, a.dt, :second)
      station_dt = DateTime.add(a.dt, round(frac * secs), :second)
      lon = a.lon_deg + frac * Angle.signed_delta(a.lon_deg, b.lon_deg)

      kind = if b.rate_deg_day < 0.0, do: "retrograde station (→ R)", else: "direct station (→ D)"
      [%{kind: kind, when_utc: DateTime.to_iso8601(station_dt), longitude_deg: Float.round(lon, 3)}]
    else
      []
    end
  end)

if stations == [] do
  Kino.Markdown.new("No station found in this window — try a wider range or a body that turns retrograde here (Mars 2024-11 → 2025-04 is a sure hit).")
else
  Kino.DataTable.new(stations, name: "#{body} stations in window")
end
```

Refinement note: this is a linear interpolation on a coarse grid. A production
pipeline seeds from exactly this kind of scan, then refines each station to
sub-minute precision with a bracketed root-find on the topocentric rate.

## 4 — Geocentric vs topocentric rate

`TopocentricMotion.ecliptic_lon_rates/4` recomputes the rate in the observer's
topocentric frame. Diurnal parallax adds a small wobble versus the geocentric
rate — negligible for outer planets, but it's the frame the grid actually
refines in, so the detectors stay self-consistent.

```elixir
# Sample near the middle of the window for a representative comparison.
mid_dt = Enum.at(sample_dts, div(length(sample_dts), 2))

topo_rates = TopocentricMotion.ecliptic_lon_rates(mid_dt, location, [body])
{:ok, mid_obs} = SnapshotPipeline.observe(mid_dt, location, [body])
geo_rate = mid_obs.motion[body].ecliptic_lon_rate_deg_per_day
topo_rate = topo_rates[body]

Kino.Markdown.new("""
At **#{DateTime.to_iso8601(mid_dt)}** for **#{body}**:

| Frame | Rate (°/day) |
|-------|--------------|
| Geocentric (pipeline) | #{Float.round(geo_rate, 6)} |
| Topocentric (central diff) | #{Float.round(topo_rate, 6)} |
| Difference | #{Float.round((topo_rate - geo_rate) * 3600, 3)} ″/day |
""")
```

```elixir
# augment/5 folds the topocentric rate onto the geocentric motion map.
augmented = TopocentricMotion.augment(mid_dt, location, [body], mid_obs.motion)
Kino.DataTable.new([Map.put(augmented[body], :body, body)], name: "Augmented motion map for #{body}")
```

## Pitfalls & notes

1. **Retrograde is geocentric** — it's a line-of-sight effect from Earth's own
   motion, so it barely depends on the observer. Topocentric parallax only
   perturbs the *rate* slightly; it doesn't create or move the loop.

2. **Rate sign is the definition** — `retrograde: true` ⇔ negative ecliptic
   longitude rate. Stations are the zero crossings, nothing more exotic.

3. **`ecliptic_lon_rates/4` is central difference** — it calls `observe/4` at
   `datetime ± delta_minutes` (default 30). Very near a station the second
   derivative matters, so tighten `delta_minutes:` if you refine there.

4. **Coarse grids miss fast bodies** — Mercury's whole retrograde is ~3 weeks;
   drop the step to 1 day (or less) so you don't skip the stations.

5. **`augment/5` mutates only the rate key** — it adds
   `:topocentric_ecliptic_lon_rate_deg_per_day` and leaves the rest of each
   body's geocentric motion map intact.

## Key ideas

* **The planet never stops** — the rate curve dipping through zero is a parallax
  artifact of Earth's orbit, not celestial mechanics grinding to a halt.
* **Stations are zero crossings** — retrograde detection reduces to a sign change
  on one number the pipeline already emits. The loop plot is the payoff.
* **Coarse scan → refine** — this notebook mirrors a common pattern: coarse
  `async_stream` scan, then bracket and refine each station.
